 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "whl_baird",
   "title": "John Logie Baird",
   "text": "John Logie Baird was born in 1888 in Helensburgh, a few miles west of here on the Firth of Clyde. Often ill and usually broke, he kept inventing anyway, and on 26 January 1926, in an attic in Frith Street, Soho, he showed moving television pictures to members of the Royal Institution. The BBC later chose a rival electronic system, and Baird's mechanical one faded out."
  },
  {
   "text": "BALLOCH. West: Loch Lomond, the Trossachs and on to Oban. East: Glasgow. The pier is for the paddle steamer and the car park is for everyone else.",
   "x": 60,
   "y": 27
  },
  {
   "text": "FINGERPOST. Luss, north. Oban, west. Ben Lomond, back the way you came and then up, a long way up.",
   "x": 13,
   "y": 19
  },
  {
   "text": "BALLOCH PIER. The Maid of the Loch is moored here. Volunteers are still restoring her, so come in and say hello.",
   "x": 41,
   "y": 29
  },
  {
   "text": "BEN LOMOND SUMMIT. The view takes in the loch, the Trossachs and, on a clear day, a mountain you can't name that someone is already naming loudly.",
   "x": 54,
   "y": 4
  },
  {
   "text": "LOCH LOMOND. By surface area it is the biggest stretch of fresh water in Great Britain: about 24 miles long, with about thirty islands. In the song the low road is the way the spirit of the dead returns home, which is why he gets there first.",
   "x": 44,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "whl_loch_lomond",
   "title": "Loch Lomond"
  },
  {
   "text": "BEN LOMOND. At 974 metres it is the most southerly Munro. A Munro is a Scottish mountain over 3,000 feet, named for Sir Hugh Munro, who first listed them in 1891. Ticking them all off is called Munro-bagging, and it is not as violent as it sounds.",
   "x": 55,
   "y": 6,
   "plaque": "whl_ben_lomond",
   "title": "Ben Lomond"
  },
  {
   "text": "WEST HIGHLAND WAY. Scotland's first official long distance path, opened in 1980. It runs 96 miles from Milngavie, north of Glasgow, to Fort William, and the lochside stretch is where most people discover that their boots are not as waterproof as the shop said.",
   "x": 30,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "whl_west_highland_way",
   "title": "West Highland Way"
  },
  {
   "text": "MAID OF THE LOCH. Launched in 1953, she was the last paddle steamer built in Britain. She carried passengers up and down Loch Lomond until 1981, and volunteers have been bringing her back to life ever since.",
   "x": 42,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "whl_maid_of_the_loch",
   "title": "Maid of the Loch"
  },
  {
   "text": "OSPREYS. Hunted out of Scotland as breeding birds by the early 1900s, they came back by themselves to Loch Garten in 1954. They fish the lochs all summer, diving feet first, then fly to West Africa for the winter.",
   "x": 32,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "whl_ospreys",
   "title": "Ospreys"
  },
  {
   "text": "CAPERCAILLIE. The biggest grouse in the world, and a bird with opinions. The name is Gaelic, capall-coille, 'horse of the wood'. They died out in Scotland in the 1700s, were brought back from Sweden in 1837 and are now rare again. In spring a male will challenge walkers, so look wise and back away.",
   "x": 30,
   "y": 43,
   "plaque": "whl_capercaillie",
   "title": "Capercaillie"
  },
  {
   "text": "LUSS. A village of slate-roofed cottages beside the water, built for estate and quarry workers. For years it stood in for the fictional village of Glendarroch in a Scottish television drama called Take the High Road.",
   "x": 9,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "whl_luss",
   "title": "Luss"
  },
  {
   "text": "ROB ROY'S CAVE. Rob Roy MacGregor (1671 to 1734) was a cattle dealer turned outlaw, and legend says he hid in caves on the east shore of Loch Lomond. Whether this very cave ever sheltered him is a matter for the tourist board.",
   "x": 48,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "whl_rob_roys_cave",
   "title": "Rob Roy's Cave"
  },
  {
   "text": "BALLOCH. The name comes from the Gaelic bealach, which here means the outlet of the loch: the little River Leven leaves the water just south of here and heads for the Clyde.",
   "x": 52,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "whl_balloch",
   "title": "Balloch"
  },
  {
   "text": "THE CLAN BAN. The name MacGregor was banned by law for much of the 1600s and 1700s (1603 to 1661, and again 1693 to 1774). Rob Roy sometimes went by his mother's name, Campbell, which was a joke only the MacGregors found funny.",
   "x": 24,
   "y": 42,
   "plaque": "whl_clan_ban",
   "title": "The Clan Ban"
  }
 ],
